<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Exp Med</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Experimental Medicine</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0022-1007</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-9538</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">19870825</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2133707</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>THE SIGNIFICANCE OF HUMAN DOUBLE ZONE BETA HEMOLYTIC STREPTOCOCCI IN THE UDDER OF THE COW</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Little</surname><given-names>Ralph B.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x1bf4280N0x36596e0">From the Department of Animal and Plant Pathology of The Rockefeller Institute for Medical Research, Princeton, New Jersey</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>31</day><month>10</month><year>1938</year></pub-date><volume>68</volume><issue>6</issue><fpage>905</fpage><lpage>911</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>26</day><month>7</month><year>1938</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; Copyright, 1938, by The Rockefeller Institute for Medical Research New York</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> Human strains of double zone beta hemolytic streptococci produced mastitis in 11 quarters of 4 young cows. Following the acute phase of the infection, the streptococci were eliminated from 6 quarters at various intervals. However, sufficient positive infections were maintained throughout the period of observation to show that the human strains can produce the same degree of infection as bovine double zone beta hemolytic streptococci. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>